---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ControlledEvaluationFlow from "../components/diagrams/ControlledEvaluationFlow.astro";
import QualificationNotice from "../components/QualificationNotice.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Controlled Evaluation | BeaconGuard Architecture Review"
  description="Request a bounded BeaconGuard Controlled Evaluation covering dual authorization, Verify evidence requirements, ALLOW and DENY cases, and pilot qualification findings."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Controlled Evaluation</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A Controlled Evaluation is a bounded architecture review for one regulated
      AI workflow. It validates Request Admission Authorization,
      Result-to-Action Authorization, Verify investigation requirements, and
      deployment fit—without replacing existing systems of record or claiming
      customer production authorization.
    </p>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard authorizes whether an enterprise application may invoke an AI
      provider and whether a later declared enterprise action may proceed under
      the Active Verified Release. An AI-generated result remains an untrusted
      proposal. Provider success is not enterprise authorization.
    </p>
  </section>

  <QualificationNotice variant="full" />

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Not a rip-and-replace proposal</h2>
    <p>
      Existing applications continue to own workflow logic and records.
      BeaconGuard is evaluated as the independent authorization and verification
      layer for the governed AI transaction. BeaconGuard authorizes; the
      enterprise application executes; the enterprise system of record remains
      authoritative.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Inputs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>One bounded regulated AI workflow</li>
      <li>Representative outbound request</li>
      <li>Representative provider response</li>
      <li>Intended enterprise action</li>
      <li>Request–result–action correlation requirements</li>
      <li>Identity and tenant context</li>
      <li>Active Verified Release assumptions</li>
      <li>Request ALLOW case</li>
      <li>Request DENY case</li>
      <li>Result ALLOW case (declared action authorized)</li>
      <li>Result DENY case (declared action denied)</li>
      <li>Evidence requirements</li>
      <li>Reconstruction requirements</li>
      <li>Replay requirements (investigation)</li>
      <li>Report requirements</li>
      <li>Export requirements</li>
      <li>Deployment boundaries</li>
      <li>Failure handling</li>
      <li>Non-AI fallback behavior</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Controlled Evaluation workflow</h2>
    <ControlledEvaluationFlow />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Activities</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Dual-boundary placement for request admission and declared action</li>
      <li>System Connector fit where applicable</li>
      <li>Identity, tenant, and environment mapping</li>
      <li>Request–result–action correlation review</li>
      <li>Compliance Playbook and Active Verified Release fit</li>
      <li>Request ALLOW and DENY case design</li>
      <li>Result-to-Action ALLOW and DENY case design</li>
      <li>
        Post-DENY governance review scenario design (review does not mutate a
        DENY into ALLOW)
      </li>
      <li>Evidence, reconstruction, and investigation-replay review</li>
      <li>Report provenance and verifiable-export review</li>
      <li>AI-assisted insights non-authority confirmation</li>
      <li>Deployment boundary and bypass-prevention review</li>
      <li>Failure handling and non-AI fallback review</li>
      <li>Responsibility separation and residual-risk review</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section" aria-labelledby="controlled-demo-card-title">
    <div class="content-card proof-demo-card">
      <p class="section-label">CONTROLLED DEMONSTRATION</p>
      <h2 id="controlled-demo-card-title">See the BeaconGuard Review Process in Operation</h2>
      <p>
        Watch a controlled example of deterministic denial, reviewer step-up
        authentication, bounded one-time authorization, a new governed request,
        and rejection of authorization reuse.
      </p>
      <p>
        The demonstration proves immutable original denial, step-up reviewer
        authentication, a new governed request, single-use authorization, and
        replay rejection. It is not proof of the complete dual-authorization
        transaction lifecycle.
      </p>
      <div class="page-actions">
        <a class="btn-primary" href="/proof-evidence#beaconguard-review-process">Watch the Review Process</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Outputs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Governed Transaction Architecture Memo</li>
      <li>Control Boundary Findings</li>
      <li>Evidence and Verification Findings</li>
      <li>Pilot Qualification Findings</li>
      <li>Residual Risks</li>
      <li>Unsupported Claims</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Who should participate</h2>
    <div class="section-grid section-grid--compact">
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Healthcare</h3>
        <p>
          Teams owning PHI-sensitive operational AI pathways where request
          admission and declared-action authorization must be explicit.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Financial services</h3>
        <p>
          Teams owning AML, KYC, fraud, dispute, or customer-impacting
          recommendation workflows that need dual authorization points.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <h3>Regulated internal automation</h3>
        <p>
          Teams placing agents or automations against production systems under
          fail-closed controls with Verify investigation requirements.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Contact the team</h2>
    <p>
      Timing and commercial terms are confirmed during intake. Do not submit
      PHI, customer records, credentials, production secrets, or confidential
      evidence in the initial message.
    </p>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/contact">Request Controlled Evaluation</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/platform">Review platform overview</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
